\ Compute-once words on bare metal. The cell rides the runtime image as a slot the loader fills,
\ and the force calls the body's compiled function: there is no interpreter here to run the
\ stored bytecode, so a cell without a compiled body could not be computed at all.
\
\ Two call sites over one cell is the property under test, and the body announcing itself is how
\ that becomes visible. A lowering that gave each site its own cell would print the first line
\ twice; the greeting alone would look the same either way.
\
\ The hosted force also parks a second reader and names a cycle across several words. Neither is
\ reachable from here: `spawn` raises `build-unsupported`, so one execution reaches a cell at a
\ time, and reading a cycle's diagnostic needs `recover` and `@get`, which this runtime does not
\ carry. Those stay covered by the hosted fixtures.

greeting: ( -- s ) once [ "building the greeting" print-line "computed once" ] ;

greeting print-line
greeting print-line
